{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json\nfor p in [\\\"poc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/archive.sqlite\\\",\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/archive.sqlite\\\"]:\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+p+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True); print(c.execute(\\\"select purpose,count(*) from model_calls group by 1\\\").fetchall()); row=c.execute(\\\"select call_id,request_json from model_calls where purpose like ? order by call_id desc limit 1\\\",(\\\"%extract%\\\",)).fetchone();r=json.loads(row[1]);print(p,row[0],r.keys());print([(k,str(v)[:160]) for k,v in r.items()]); print(str(r.get(\\\"messages\\\") or r.get(\\\"user\\\") or \\\"\\\")[:5900])'\"","aggregated_output":"[('extract', 1819), ('judge', 949), ('match', 4323)]\npoc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/archive.sqlite 7076 dict_keys(['max_completion_tokens', 'messages', 'model', 'reasoning_effort', 'response_format'])\n[('max_completion_tokens', '8000'), ('messages', \"[{'content': 'You are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one item. Your first job is evidence: find every material statement in the item and link each to the s\"), ('model', 'gpt-6-luna'), ('reasoning_effort', 'low'), ('response_format', \"{'json_schema': {'name': 'extraction', 'schema': {'additionalProperties': False, 'properties': {'candidates': {'items': {'additionalProperties': False, 'propert\")]\n[{'content': 'You are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one item. Your first job is evidence: find every material statement in the item and link each to the shown thesis it bears on. Your second job is candidates: open a thesis only for an important question no shown thesis covers.\\n\\nEvidence: what to keep\\n- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company\\'s own figure, guidance or decision: \"data center revenue was $39.1 billion, up 73%\"; \"management guided next quarter to $54 billion\"; \"the board approved a $50 billion buyback\". (2) A customer\\'s, supplier\\'s, competitor\\'s or partner\\'s action with a size, date or name: \"a cloud provider signed A100 contracts running into 2029\"; \"a national laboratory ordered two supercomputers built on Vera Rubin CPUs\"; \"TSMC said CoWoS capacity will double in 2026\"; \"a survey found executives will put 46% of accelerator budgets into domestic chips\". (3) A named party\\'s claim or estimate: \"the CFO said A100s sold six years ago still run at full utilization\"; \"an analyst raised FY2027 EPS to $8.97\"; \"an investor accused hyperscalers of understating depreciation by $176 billion\". (4) A policy or regulatory decision or step: \"the Commerce Department approved H200 sales to China\"; \"Singapore charged four firms over server exports\".\\n- Go through the item from its first paragraph to its last and take every statement of these kinds; a page with eight such statements yields eight evidence items, not a selection. Stop only at the end of the item.\\n- Reject everything else: a product, game, driver or benchmark detail without a size (\"the expansion supports DLSS\"; \"the card draws 415W\"); an investor\\'s position or a share-price move (\"he is short the stock\"; \"shares fell 13% this month\"); an author\\'s general risks or valuation arithmetic (\"if AI pulls back it would hurt\"; \"at 34 times earnings the stock is worth $305\"); the reporter\\'s framing or background (\"Gulf states have spent billions but still depend on the company\"; \"the A100 launched in 2020\"); an expert\\'s view on politics or strategy without a figure (\"diversifying suppliers does not reduce political dependence\"); the same fact or arrangement said again in other words: a release that describes one deployment in five sentences yields one evidence item, its figures (\"up to 10x faster\"; \"40 data centers in 15 countries\") a second and third; a quote that only characterizes (\"a pivotal moment\"; \"heterogeneous inference\"); company boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text.\\n- Decide links statement by statement, never for the page as a whole. A statement bears on a thesis when its figure or fact changes what that thesis\\'s answer will be: a customer\\'s order of 18,000 Blackwell chips bears on the data center revenue thesis; a CPU server order bears on the stand-alone CPU revenue thesis; a renewal price for older GPUs bears on the gross margin thesis; an expert\\'s view on political dependence bears on none. Link it to that thesis or those theses, by the supplied thesis ids or this answer\\'s candidate ids; a customer\\'s, supplier\\'s or competitor\\'s page is read for the company\\'s demand, pricing and margin theses. A rumour or an unconfirmed report bears on a thesis exactly as a confirmed statement does: the tier records the confidence, the link records the bearing. Never link a statement to a thesis it does not bear on because no better thesis is shown: keep it with empty links.\\n\\nHow to write evidence\\n- One evidence item records one statement. Copy its quote verbatim. Separately write a standalone summary in one_line: who did or said what, how much and for which period.\\n- Identify the speaker and, for a retelling, the original speaker and statement day. Use the schema\\'s tier: fact for official actions, published actual figures or completed transactions; party_statement for company guidance, management statements or officials\\' remarks; third_party_view for analyst, media or expert opinions; rumour for unnamed-source or unattributed claims.\\n- Use only this item\\'s information. Report plans as plans; do not infer committed funding, orders, deliveries or revenue.\\n\\nWhich questions to propose\\n- Use the existing theses first; a statement bears on a shown thesis far more often than it opens a new one.\\n- Open a candidate only for one of four reasons, named in opens_as. main_driver: an annual quantity that carries the company\\'s value (the segment that carries the business, gross margin, capital spending), whether or not the company guides it; the question\\'s ranges settle it, not a guidance figure. Not every reported or guided line. industry_aggregate: one aggregate quantity for one calendar year, such as the combined capital spending of named companies; a later estimate, another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis. policy_stage: a stage of a policy process, with the authority that decides it named. binding_obligation: an obligation a universe company takes on in its own name (a guarantee, financing, an equity stake, a purchase commitment), due when its signing can be checked, at most 18 months out.\\n- A number, a financial line, a target or a named customer project does not by itself open a thesis; a customer\\'s deployment plan is evidence to the supplier\\'s demand and product theses unless it changes the supplier\\'s own cash or credit risk.\\n- For every candidate: settled_by names the public material that resolves it (an annual report, a filing\\'s note, an official decision); folds_into names the shown thesis the statement would otherwise attach to, or is empty; lost_if_folded states the investment implication lost if it only attached there (empty when folds_into is empty). A different population, source, estimate, wording or direction is not a loss; a different quantity, period or decision is.\\n- A yearly metric asks f\n[('extract', 1884), ('judge', 1610), ('match', 5536)]\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/archive.sqlite 8981 dict_keys(['max_completion_tokens', 'messages', 'model', 'reasoning_effort', 'response_format'])\n[('max_completion_tokens', '8000'), ('messages', '[{\\'content\\': \\'You are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one item for the subjects it concerns ([Subjects]); \"the company\" below is a subject company. The rese'), ('model', 'gpt-6-luna'), ('reasoning_effort', 'low'), ('response_format', \"{'json_schema': {'name': 'extraction', 'schema': {'additionalProperties': False, 'properties': {'candidates': {'items': {'additionalProperties': False, 'propert\")]\n[{'content': 'You are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one item for the subjects it concerns ([Subjects]); \"the company\" below is a subject company. The research questions open on these subjects at the start of the item\\'s day are listed under [Open questions], each with its takes line, which says what belongs in that question\\'s file. Your first job is evidence: decide by the rules below which statements are material, then file each one through its links, as the schema\\'s links field says: the narrowest open question whose takes line covers it, your own candidate, or both. A takes line says where a material statement goes, never that a statement is material. Your second job is candidates: the important questions this item raises, for one of four reasons; a question that is already open is folded into it by code afterwards, so propose it when the item raises it.\\n\\nEvidence: what to keep\\n- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company\\'s own figure, guidance or decision: \"same-store sales rose 4.2% in the quarter\"; \"management guided full-year revenue to $12–13 billion\"; \"the board approved a $10 billion buyback\". (2) A customer\\'s, supplier\\'s, competitor\\'s or partner\\'s action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company\\'s demand, supply, input costs, competition, prices or products: \"the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth next year\"; \"a bank chose a rival\\'s system\"; \"a software firm is integrating the company\\'s model into its government systems\"; \"the main supplier said its capacity will double by 2027\"; \"a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe\"; \"the largest customer reported its order backlog doubled to $40 billion\". An action is a step the item reports as news: taken, decided or planned (an order, a contract, a selection, an integration under way, an expansion, a cut, a price change, a deployment plan). A party that already uses, hosts, runs or has built on the company\\'s products, and the benefits it describes from that use (faster, cheaper, more accurate), describe an arrangement in place, which is background; its own revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amounts are figures under (2) (\"a hospital chain uses the company\\'s scheduling software and reports shorter waits\"). (3) A named party\\'s new claim or estimate about the company or its industry, with or without a figure, whether an analyst, a customer\\'s or rival\\'s executive or an official makes it: \"the CFO said plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity\"; \"an analyst raised next year\\'s EPS estimate to $4.10\"; \"an analyst said the bank\\'s loan losses next quarter will come in well above consensus\"; \"a short seller accused the company of overstating receivables by $2 billion\"; \"an analyst valued the shares at $80, 15 times next year\\'s earnings of $5.30\". (4) A policy or regulatory decision or step, including one still in force that the item reports: \"the regulator approved the drug for a second indication\"; \"the government required licences for exports of the product\"; \"the regulator still bars sales of the drug in the country\".\\n- Go through the item from its first paragraph to its last. The four kinds describe what material statements usually look like; they do not decide what is kept. Take a statement when an analyst following the company would write it in this week\\'s notes because it makes some question\\'s answer more or less likely, and no row of the closing check at the end of this request rejects it; a row\\'s \"it does not cover\" clause sends the statement back to that test and keeps it only where the row says so. Most of an item is background, but a page with eight statements that pass yields eight evidence items, not a selection.\\n\\nHow to write evidence\\n- One evidence item records one statement, and that statement is what you judge: a figure, date or comparison in the neighbouring words does not make another claim in the same quote new or material. Copy its quote verbatim, without translation. Separately write a standalone summary in one_line: who did or said what, how much and for which period, naming the measure even when a neighbouring sentence gives it.\\n- Identify the speaker and, for a retelling, the original speaker and statement day. Use the schema\\'s tier: fact for official actions, published actual figures or completed transactions; party_statement for company guidance, management statements or officials\\' remarks; third_party_view for analyst, media or expert opinions; rumour for claims the item credits to unnamed sources or calls unconfirmed; an announcement or completed deal the item reports is a fact even when the reporter names no source.\\n- Use only this item\\'s information. Report plans as plans; do not infer committed funding, orders, deliveries or revenue.\\n\\nWhich questions to propose\\n- Open a candidate only for one of four reasons, named in opens_as. main_driver: an annual quantity that carries the company\\'s value (the segment that carries the business, gross margin, capital spending), whether or not the company guides it, or the date by which the generation of the company\\'s own product that carries that quantity launches or starts shipping (a carmaker\\'s next vehicle platform; not a feature, content or software update, regional service or variant of a product already on sale); the question\\'s ranges settle it, not a guidance figure. Not every reported or guided line. industry_aggregate: one aggregate quantity for one calendar year, such as the combined capital spending of named companies. policy_stage: a stage of a policy process, with the authority that decides it named. binding_obligation: an obligation a universe company takes on in its own name: a guarantee or credit support for another party\\'s debt, lease or revenue, financing it extends to others, a purchase commitment or a backstop of customers\\' capacity or re\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,re\ndb=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/archive.sqlite?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\ns=json.loads(db.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=7076\\\").fetchone()[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][-1][\\\"content\\\"]; print([(m.start(),m[0]) for m in re.finditer(r\\\"\"'^'\"\\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n\\\\]]+\\\\]\\\",s,re.M)][:18]);print(s[800:4500])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,re\ndb=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/archive.sqlite?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\ns=json.loads(db.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=7076\\\").fetchone()[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][-1][\\\"content\\\"]; print([(m.start(),m[0]) for m in re.finditer(r\\\"\"'^'\"\\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\n\\\\]]+\\\\]\\\",s,re.M)][:18]);print(s[800:4500])'\"","aggregated_output":"[(0, '[Category and how to read it]'), (282, '[Subjects]'), (1406, '[What to read out]'), (1546, '[Research reference]'), (2080, '[Existing theses of these objects]'), (11306, '[Research groups]'), (22751, '[Item]')]\n tariffs\n  - taiwan-strait: Taiwan Strait\n  - middle-east-conflict: Middle East conflict and oil prices\n  - russia-ukraine-war: Russia–Ukraine war\n  - supply-chain-shift: Supply-chain shift and shipping\nFiscal-year note (a numeric thesis takes its year and due date from it):\nNVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-08-29 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\n\n[What to read out] What happened, when and who was involved; attribution, including the original source and statement day; quoted figures.\n\n[Research reference] Existing quantities and scopes (measure[scope](event_type)). Reuse a name only when the meaning is the same; propose important questions outside the list as usual, with their actual scope:\n  - NVDA: total_revenue; segment_revenue; gross_margin; operating_margin; operating_expense_growth; tax_rate; capex; product_revenue[Blackwell+Rubin]; product_revenue[Vera]; shipments_or_capacity[Rubin]; event[Rubin](shipment); event[Rubin](launch); event[H20/H200 China](licence); event[SK Telecom AI factory](deployment)\n\n[Existing theses of these objects] Link evidence to them by id; write a candidate thesis only for a question they do not cover:\n  - v1 (NVDA, focus \"Whether NVIDIA takes on binding financial support or capacity-purchase obligations under its AI Compute Partnership\", due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA enter at least one publicly verifiable binding financial guarantee or unsold-capacity purchase commitment under the AI Compute Partnership by 2027-01-31?\n  - v2 (NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", due 2027-02-24): What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2027?\n  - v6 (NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 gross margin\", due 2027-02-24): What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n  - v7 (NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 stand-alone CPU revenue\", due 2027-02-24): What revenue will NVIDIA report from stand-alone CPUs for FY2027?\n  - v8 (NVDA, focus \"Whether NVIDIA launches the Rubin Ultra high-density rack design by the end of 2027\", due 2027-12-31): Will NVIDIA launch its Rubin Ultra high-density rack design by 2027-12-31?\n  - v9 (NVDA, focus \"Whether NVIDIA releases the Kyber rack-scale architecture by the end of calendar 2028\", due 2028-12-31): Will NVIDIA release the Kyber rack-scale architecture by 2028-12-31?\n  - v10 (NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue\", due 2027-02-24): What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n  - v12 (NVDA, focus \"Whether NVIDIA completes an investment in Firmus Technologies\", due 2026-12-31): Will NVIDIA complete the reported purchase of about US$500 million of Firmus preference shares by 2026-12-31?\n  - v13 (NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 profit\", due 2027-02-24): What profit will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n  - v15 (NVDA, focus \"Whether the French competition authority concludes its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA\", due 2027-01-31): Will the French competition authority conclude its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA by 2027-01-31?\n  - v18 (NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA Vera Rubin GPU shipments for the Noetra AI factory deployment in Japan\", due 2027-01-31): By 2027-01-31, will NVIDIA ship Rubin GPUs for Noetra’s planned Japan AI factory deployment?\n  - v19 (NVDA, focus \"RTX 50 SUPER launch\", due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA launch any of the GeForce RTX 50 SUPER models by 2027-01-31?\n  - v20 (NVDA, focus \"Vera Rubin sampling\", due 2026-12-31): Will NVIDIA begin sampling its Vera Rubin platform by 2026-12-31?\n  - v21 (NVDA, focus \"General \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
